  data:  # b90d
    kind: work_item  # b90d
    title: "access_mode applicability matrix: classify the ~37 uncensused edge types (+ script_src) via a polyglot census (ADR-0038 ruling 2 tail)"  # b90d
    status: todo_soft  # b90d
    priority: 3  # b90d
    parent:  # b90d
    tags:  # b90d
    - access_mode  # b90d
    - schema  # b90d
    - vocab  # b90d
    isbefore: []  # b90d
    duplicate_of: []  # b90d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b90d
    pr_ref:  # b90d
    description: "PR 1 of ADR-0038 ruling 2 (dev 880cb6775a) landed the access_mode applicability matrix for INV-tibob's 17-type census (axis_meta_keys._ACCESS_MODE_APPLICABLE_EDGE_TYPES / _ACCESS_MODE_NA_EDGE_TYPES): 4 applicable (calls/references/module_attr_ref/event_publishes) + 12 Declared-N/A (11 structural + instantiates). But the current canonical vocabulary is 54 edge types (edge_types.all_edge_type_names()), so ~37 remain UNCLASSIFIED — the matrix declares nothing for them, so a consumer reading access_mode=None on e.g. an http_calls edge cannot tell 'does not arise' from 'missing data' (INV-tibob's interpretability gap persists for them).\n\nThe ~37 don't appear in Python self-analysis, so classifying them needs a POLYGLOT census. Known groupings: call-family (http_calls, grpc_calls, abi_call, subprocess_calls, template_calls, caller_invokes, invokes_callback, kernel_launch) — likely applicable by the ADR's 'calls is applicable' logic, but each needs a real emitter check; structural/containment/inheritance/dependency/routing/template (association, base_image, build_tag_alternative_of, constrains, contains_routes, defines_target, depends, extends_template, includes, includes_class, includes_template, links, links_to, module_exports, notifies_resource, openapi_implements, renders, requires_resource, resolver_for_type, resolver_implements, signal_receiver, sources, uses_mixin, uses_vocabulary, wraps, implements_rpc, graphql_calls) — likely N/A. Two EXCLUSIONS that are NOT this item: crypto_flow + data_flows_to carry dataflow DIRECTION and belong to ADR-0038 ruling 3 / PR-2's data_direction key, NOT access_mode; and script_src is a census structural type deferred here because it is on the mid-fold endpoint_shape axis (ADR-0023) — fold it in once endpoint_shape settles.\n\nCLOSE CONDITION (WI-nibis discipline — do NOT close on Python self-analysis alone, the 37 are invisible there): a substrate-wide NEGATIVE close-check over a polyglot cohort confirming (a) every emitted edge type is classified applicable XOR na, and (b) no type declared applicable is silently 0%-populated by a live emitter. Treat as a human-ruled reconciliation (INV-pubom amend-by-explicit-ruling precedent), not agent-discretion axis classification. Parent: INV-tibob."  # b90d
    fields: {}  # b90d
- op: discuss  # 723b
  at: '2026-07-04T14:53:44Z'  # 723b
  by: agent  # 723b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 723b
  clock: 2  # 723b
  nonce: 723b  # 723b
  message: "GATED on the endpoint_shape->relationship axis fold — empirical finding (NOT ready-to-execute; stays todo_soft, parked). Census of the 54 canonical edge types: 4 applicable + 12 na already classified; 38 unclassified = 35 in-scope + 3 excluded (crypto_flow/data_flows_to -> ADR-0038 ruling 3 data_direction; script_src -> endpoint_shape). Of the 35 in-scope the axis distribution is 23 endpoint_shape + 8 relationship + 4 pending_classification.\n\nThe _ACCESS_MODE_APPLICABLE/NA_EDGE_TYPES sets are relationship-axis consumer sets; edge_types.find_axis_drift(strict=True) forbids any member whose axis is not in {relationship, pending_classification}. Classifying the 23 endpoint_shape types into these sets is the axis-purity violation the strict drift linter is built to catch — they MUST wait for the endpoint_shape->relationship fold (ADR-0023 mid-fold). (The CI property test at test_edge_types.py:142 runs find_axis_drift NON-strict, so it would not trip today, but the strict pre-commit linter would, and the axis-declaration discipline forbids the mix.) The item's close condition wants a TOTAL census (every EMITTED type classified applicable XOR na) — impossible while 23/35 are blocked, so a partial classification of the 12 relationship/pending types would not close it.\n\nThe human-ruled fork (the 6 LIVE call-family types applicable-vs-N/A) is also mostly moot: 5 of the 6 (abi_call, caller_invokes, template_calls, invokes_callback, kernel_launch) are endpoint_shape (blocked); only subprocess_calls is relationship. So the ruling defers with the census.\n\nRE-SCOPE: Phase-3-gated, prerequisite = the endpoint_shape->relationship fold. Revisit after that fold; then the call-family applicable-vs-N/A ruling (recommend N/A-now + a separate 'stamp access_mode on synthetic call-family edges' follow-on, per ADR-0038's per-analyzer-as-dataflow-matures precedent) becomes actionable. Finding produced during the WI-hotug/WI-huhum/WI-sigam/WI-jusus campaign (design workflow wf_c1977307-761 + empirical axis check)."  # 723b
